From aff58da21bfa569cbbfc2ec6c41982f7215683dc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 17:55:20 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09296/11362] Probe the backend the agent will actually use,
 and stop declaring MTP we do not run

Kernel v27 confirmed the vLLM backend engages on the scored card, but left the one
thing that matters unproven: whether an induction round-trips through the response
translation layer. The pre-flight probe could not answer that, because it probes
llama.cpp -- it launches a second llama-server, loads the 23 GB GGUF, and generates
against a process the vLLM path never touches. So v27 printed "GENERATOR HEALTHY"
about the wrong server, and printed a concurrency failure about it too.

That concurrency banner was a false alarm on its own terms, and had been for a while.
It asks for CARNOT_ARC_INDUCE_MAX_TOKENS (131072) generated tokens under a 900 s
ceiling; the probe log recorded 24810 tokens at 28.11 tok/s per slot, so 900 s buys
roughly 25 k tokens and the request cannot finish. Arithmetic, not a serving fault.

The probe now forks on the backend. The vLLM branch calls the agent's own
_ensure_vllm_server and _vllm_raw_completion rather than hand-rolled HTTP, because
the translation layer is the only unproven part and a hand-rolled probe can pass
while it is broken. Admission is checked by reading max_model_len instead of
generating to the cap -- vLLM pages KV, so the static-pool-divided-K-ways truncation
the llama.cpp probe defends against has no equivalent here. Generation is short (256
tokens) but the prompt is worst-case sized, because prompt size is what admission
turns on. The server is left running, so the agent reuses it and the probe costs one
model load rather than two.

Separately, scored MTP goes back to "0". Commit e7b3628d88 set it to "1" for the
NVFP4 GGUF's baked MTP layers and broke test_arc_submitted_agent_parity, which pins
the contract as a literal; that regression went unnoticed until now, and v27 reported
the config/run disagreement itself. "0" is correct on both paths at once: the scored
path is vLLM, which configures no speculation, and the llama.cpp fallback measured
FASTER without it at this eval's concurrency (228.3 tok/s at k=16 against 108.8 with
MTP on -- speculation and batching compete for the same compute). The "~1.4x win" the
old comments cite was single-stream on gemma with a real draft head; measured in a
regime the scored run does not operate in.

Two tests pinned the same constant to opposite values, which is not double coverage.
The migration-defects test now pins separate NAMES, which was always the property
worth protecting -- two questions may share an answer without becoming one question.
The exp4754 fixture derives model and MTP from the live constants instead of naming
gemma-4-31B-it, which went stale at the Qwen3.8-27B migration.

Verified: emitted agent source compiles; git diff -w shows zero deletions, so the
reindent changed no content. Against HEAD over the same selection: 1 test fixed, 0
broken. Six failures remain in the exp4744/exp4754 readiness-gate family, all red at
HEAD before this change and all traceable to the same stale migration -- reported,
not swept in here.

